{
  "image_name": "2b104421d25549e637e2e33f0e25294c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different collagen types are organized in cartilage and how they change during cartilage development. Overall, it shows a hierarchy from collagen amino acid chains to microfibrils, fibrils, fibres, the extracellular matrix, and finally whole tissue, emphasizing that distinct combinations of collagen types build different cartilage structures such as hyaline cartilage and fibrocartilage.\n\nA central theme is the spatial organization of the cartilage matrix around chondrocytes. The matrix appears divided into interterritorial, territorial, and pericellular regions, each enriched with different components. Thick collagen fibres form a broader framework in the interterritorial matrix, while finer type II fibrils dominate the territorial matrix. Around the chondrocyte, the pericellular matrix seems to contain a specialized network including type VI and type IV collagen, along with proteoglycan-rich molecules such as aggrecan and hyaluronan. This highlights that cartilage is not uniform but has layered microenvironments around cells.\n\nThe developmental panel links these structures to chondrogenesis: mesenchymal stem cells condense, differentiate into chondrocytes, and later undergo hypertrophy. Different collagens appear at different stages, with type I associated earlier, type VI during condensation, and multiple cartilage-associated collagens predominating in mature chondrocytes and hypertrophic stages.",
